WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.662
It is tempting to use an agent for every project, but that usually makes the system harder to build and harder to test.

00:00:07.662 --> 00:00:11.967
Use normal software when the next step is already known.

00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:19.192
If a business rule says a value must be between zero and one hundred, write a normal validation rule.

00:00:19.192 --> 00:00:25.602
If a user is not authorized to access a record, enforce that in application code.

00:00:25.602 --> 00:00:32.097
If the workflow is always step one, step two, step three, you may not need an agent at all.

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:37.631
Use an agent when the next step genuinely depends on what was just observed.

00:00:37.631 --> 00:00:40.731
Maybe several approved tools could help.

00:00:40.731 --> 00:00:44.676
Maybe retrieval sometimes fails and needs correction.

00:00:44.676 --> 00:00:49.556
Maybe the wording is ambiguous enough that semantic judgment is useful.

00:00:49.556 --> 00:00:54.126
For example, calculating sales tax is deterministic.

00:00:54.126 --> 00:00:56.451
A normal function is appropriate.

00:00:56.451 --> 00:01:03.898
Deciding whether a user question needs a policy document, an order database, or both may require routing.

00:01:04.348 --> 00:01:10.475
A good design principle is this: deterministic decisions should stay deterministic.

00:01:10.475 --> 00:01:16.409
Use model-driven decisions only where flexible language understanding adds real value.
